found"},{"bytes":[46],"logprob":-0.9260396957397461,"token":"."}],"message":{"content":"Echo State Networks (ESNs) are a type of recurrent neural network used for processing time-series data. They consist of a large, fixed-size, randomly connected hidden layer called the \"reservoir,\" which processes input data. The weights of the reservoir are typically fixed, and only the output weights are trained. Training involves adjusting the output weights to map the reservoir's activations to the desired output. Reservoirs in ESNs are often trained using random connectivity to ensure they can capture a wide range of temporal dynamics.\n\nSummarizing the arXiv papers, none directly match the query \"echo state networks for anomaly detection.\" The closest matches are about graph neural networks and tabular data anomaly detection, not ESNs. \n\nFinal summary: ESNs are used for time-series processing with a fixed, randomly connected reservoir layer. They are not directly related to the papers found.","role":"assistant"},"model":"qwen2.5:7b","prompt_eval_count":1060,"prompt_eval_duration":45301000,"total_duration":2108433900}}